! The machinery case for P26 (electrostriction): silicon on a small **closed**
! k-grid, with the symmetrisation switched off.
!
! Two things make this input what it is. The grid is **unshifted**, so it is
! closed under the point group and `nosym` is safe -- which matters more here
! than anywhere else, because the object P26 differentiates carries a field
! label and a strain label at once and there is no average written for it
! (`pypresso/response/electrostriction.py` refuses a reduced wedge by name).
! And it is deliberately **small and under-converged**: 2x2x2 at ecutwfc = 12
! gives an epsilon_infinity of 56 rather than silicon's 13.8, which is useless
! as physics and perfectly good for the identities the tests here check --
! the variational energy against `dielec.f90`, the cubic form of a rank-4
! tensor, and a third derivative against a finite difference of a second one.
!
! The physics numbers come from si-epsilon-unshifted-nosym.in, which is the
! same cell on a 4x4x4 grid at ecutwfc = 18 and reproduces ph.x's 13.8067.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.20, nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 12.0
    nosym = .true.
    noinv = .true.
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 2 2 2 0 0 0
